IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wills-dates`
IMPORT `wills-rules`

-- Wills Act 2000 (NT) -- the questions of the PERSON WITH AN INTEREST IN A DECEASED PERSON'S WILL.
--
-- SEAT: a beneficiary, the surviving spouse, a child or other relative, a person entitled on intestacy, a creditor, the
-- executor named in the will as a person seeking the will (not as a decision-maker); and the relative applying for a
-- Court-authorised will for someone who lacks testamentary capacity. "Can I see the will?", "Can the Court accept an
-- unsigned document as the will?", "I witnessed the will: can I keep my gift?", "Can the will be rectified, and by when must
-- I apply?", "What becomes of a gift to someone who died within 30 days?", "Can the executor pay me maintenance now?". The
-- Court, the Registrar, the prescribed person and the personal representative as decision-maker get no seat: their
-- satisfactions, orders and choices are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of wills-rules.l4.
-- No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL COPY: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word reprint REPW007 of the Wills Act
-- 2000 (NT), as in force at 1 January 2022, not an official version. Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned
-- by the Northern Territory. Quoted under the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October
-- 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Wills Act 2000 (NT): the family's and beneficiaries' questions`

§§ `Getting and seeing the will`

-- Arises after a death.

@export Am I entitled to inspect and copy the deceased's will (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 54)? Whoever holds the will (including a revoked will, a copy or a document claimed to be a will) must let you see and copy it, at your own expense, if you are named in it, the surviving spouse or issue, a parent or guardian, entitled on intestacy, a creditor or claimant, a beneficiary of an earlier will, or the parent or guardian of a minor named in it or entitled on intestacy.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who I am to the deceased` @desc Who you are to the deceased: executor named in the will; entitled to apply for letters of administration with the will annexed; named or referred to in the will; surviving spouse; child, grandchild or other issue; parent or guardian; entitled on intestacy; creditor or claimant against the estate; beneficiary of an earlier will; parent or guardian of a minor named or entitled; de facto partner not named and not entitled on intestacy; or someone else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I see the will` MEANS
    `s 54 -- I am entitled to inspect and copy the will` `who`

@export Can I have the deceased's deposited will given to me (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 51(3), (4))? The executor named in it, or a person entitled to apply for letters of administration with the will annexed, may apply in writing to the prescribed person (the Public Trustee unless another person is prescribed), who must hand it over.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who I am to the deceased` @desc Who you are to the deceased: executor named in the will; entitled to apply for letters of administration with the will annexed; or one of the other relationships listed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have the deposited will` MEANS
    `s 51(3), (4) -- I can have the deposited will given to me` `who`

§§ `Whether the will stands as written`

-- Arises after a death.

@export Can the Supreme Court accept a document that was not properly signed and witnessed as the deceased's will, a change to it, or its revocation (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 10)? Yes if the document purports to set out the deceased's wishes for their estate and the Court is satisfied they intended it to be their will, change or revocation. A document can be a note, an electronic record, a recording or a photograph, made in or outside the Territory.
GIVEN `executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document was in fact signed and witnessed as the Act requires
      `purports` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document purports to set out the deceased's testamentary intentions (their wishes for their property after death)
      `satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the deceased intended the document to be their will, a change to it, or its revocation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court accept the document as the will` MEANS
    `s 10 -- the Court may accept the document as the will` `executed` `purports` `satisfied`

@export I witnessed the will and it leaves me something: is my gift void (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 12)? A gift to a witness is void as far as it concerns them, unless at least two of the witnesses take nothing under the will, everyone who would gain from the gift failing consents in writing, or the Court is satisfied the will-maker knew and approved of it and made it freely.
GIVEN `witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the beneficiary) were one of the witnesses to the will
      `other witnesses` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the witnesses take nothing under the will
      `consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether everyone who would benefit directly if your gift failed has consented in writing to it going to you as the will says
      `knew and approved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the will-maker knew and approved of the gift to you
      `freely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the will-maker made the gift freely and voluntarily
GIVETH A `What becomes of a gift to a witness`
`Is my gift as a witness void` MEANS
    `s 12 -- what becomes of a gift to a witness` `witness` `other witnesses` `consent` `knew and approved` `freely`

§§ `Rectifying the will`

-- Arises after a death.

@export Can the Supreme Court rectify the deceased's will to carry out their intentions (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 27(1))? Only where it fails to carry them out because of a clerical error or because it does not give effect to their instructions, and the Court is satisfied of that.
GIVEN `reason` IS A `Why the will does not carry out the will-maker's intentions` @desc Why the will does not carry out the deceased's intentions: a clerical error; it does not give effect to their instructions; or some other reason
      `satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the will does not carry out the deceased's intentions for that reason
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the will be rectified` MEANS
    `s 27(1) -- the Court may rectify the will` `reason` `satisfied`

@export By what date must I apply to rectify the will (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 27(2))? Within 6 months after the date of the will-maker's death; the last day is the same date 6 months later, or the last day of that month if it is shorter.
GIVEN `death` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for rectification` MEANS
    `s 27(2) -- the last day to apply for rectification` `death`

@export Is my application to rectify the will in time, or can it still be made (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 27(2)-(4))? You must apply within 6 months after the death; the Court may extend that time if it thinks it just, before or after it ends, but not once the estate has been finally distributed.
GIVEN `death` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied to the Court for rectification
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The date you applied (if you have not, any date: it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has extended the time for your application
      `distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the final distribution of the estate has been made
GIVETH A `Where my rectification application stands`
`Is my rectification application in time` MEANS
    `s 27(2)-(4) -- where my rectification application stands` `death` `applied` `applied on` `today` `extended` `distributed`

§§ `A beneficiary who dies before, or soon after, the will-maker`

-- Arises after a death.

@export What becomes of a gift to a beneficiary who died before the will-maker or within 30 days after (Wills Act 2000 (NT) ss 30, 34, 40, 41)? A beneficiary must survive the will-maker by 30 days. If they do not, a gift to the will-maker's child or other issue goes to that beneficiary's own issue who survive by 30 days; a failed fractional share of the residue goes to the other shares; any other failed gift falls into the residue, unless the will says otherwise.
GIVEN `alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary is still alive
      `will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died
      `beneficiary died` IS A DATE @desc The date the beneficiary died (if still alive, any date: it is not used)
      `contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows a contrary intention about what happens if the beneficiary dies first (a general condition that they survive the will-maker, or reach an age, is not enough)
      `issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary was the will-maker's child, grandchild or other descendant
      `own issue survived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary left children or other descendants who survived the will-maker by 30 days
      `excluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift was to the will-maker's issue as a class without limit of remoteness, or was an interest that ends at or before the beneficiary's death (a life interest)
      `condition unmet` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will imposed a condition on the beneficiary that they had not fulfilled
      `fractional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift was a fractional share (for example one third) of the whole estate or of the residue
      `residue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift was the whole residue of the estate (not a fractional share of it)
GIVETH A `What becomes of the gift`
`What becomes of the gift to a beneficiary who died` MEANS
    `ss 30, 34, 40, 41 -- what becomes of the gift` `alive` `will-maker died` `beneficiary died` `contrary` `issue` `own issue survived` `excluded` `condition unmet` `fractional` `residue`

@export I depended on the deceased: can the executor pay me maintenance now, before my gift becomes absolute 30 days after the death (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 55)? The personal representative may (but need not) pay for your maintenance, support or education in that period, even if a family provision claim is pending; the payment is deducted from your share.
GIVEN `dependent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were wholly or substantially dependent on the deceased
      `not yet absolute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your entitlement under the will becomes absolute only 30 days after the death (because you must survive the deceased by 30 days)
      `death` IS A DATE @desc The date the deceased died
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the executor pay me maintenance now` MEANS
    `s 55 -- the personal representative may pay me maintenance now` `dependent` `not yet absolute` `death` `today`

§§ `A will for a relative who lacks capacity`

-- Arises only where a relative or friend has lost, or never had, testamentary capacity.

@export Can the Supreme Court give me leave to apply for a will to be made for my relative who lacks testamentary capacity (Wills Act 2000 (NT) ss 19-22)? Only while they are alive, and the Court must refuse leave unless satisfied of five things; even then it decides whether to grant it.
GIVEN `alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your relative is alive
      `incapable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied there is reason to believe your relative is or may be incapable of making a will
      `would have made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the proposed will is or might be one your relative would have made with testamentary capacity
      `appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied it is or may be appropriate to make an order
      `appropriate applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you are an appropriate person to apply
      `represented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied adequate steps were taken to let everyone with a legitimate interest, including those expecting a gift, be represented
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court give leave for a will for my relative` MEANS
    `ss 19(3), 21 -- the Court may give leave for a will for my relative` `alive` `incapable` `would have made` `appropriate` `appropriate applicant` `represented`
